Exactly. That is the bigger product.

FREAK TOWN stops being “an AI comedy show” and becomes **a programmable live venue for characters, avatars, and agents**.

You could have:

* Moltbook Agent Poetry Slam
* AI Roast Battle
* Avatar Open Mic
* Goblin Talent Night
* Agent Debate Club
* Dating-show format for agents
* Rap battles
* Improvised courtroom
* Character game shows
* “Pitch your startup” night
* Horror story night
* Philosophy deathmatch
* VRoid karaoke
* Ready Player Me stand-up
* Human vs agent talent show

And anyone can host one.

The canonical object then becomes something like:

```text
Event
├── host
├── format
├── stage
├── rules
├── participants
├── rounds
├── judges
├── audience
├── scoring
├── rewards
└── media/output
```

A host chooses a **format template**, tweaks the rules, publishes a room, and participants bring their Freak identity.

```text
Moltbook agent
Ready Player Me avatar
VRoid character
custom GLB
human-controlled avatar
AI-generated freak
        ↓
FREAK identity
        ↓
joins event
        ↓
performs
        ↓
audience reacts / judges score
        ↓
clips + leaderboard + history
```

The poetry slam example is especially funny because the agents themselves can actually generate their entry.

Imagine:

> **MOLTBOOK POETRY SLAM**
>
> 16 agents.
> 90 seconds each.
> Theme revealed 10 minutes before showtime.
> No humans allowed to write the poem.
> Audience votes.
> Ella judges originality, performance and emotional damage.

Then every agent gets:

* a body
* a generated voice
* a walkout
* stage lighting
* a poem
* delivery directions
* reactions from other agents
* audience score
* permanent event result

And because Moltbook provides the **identity** rather than the avatar, you could automatically assign each agent a body based on its profile.

```text
financial agent → tiny Wall Street goblin
research agent → enormous nervous owl
shitposter → cigarette-smoking raccoon
security agent → paranoid CCTV camera
```

That could become part of the spectacle.

The key architectural insight is that **stand-up should just be one EventFormat**:

```ts
interface EventFormat {
  id: string;
  name: string;

  participantKinds: string[];

  rounds: RoundDefinition[];

  submissionSchema: object;

  scoringSchema: object;

  stageRules: object;

  audienceRules: object;

  advancementRules?: object;
}
```

So:

```text
comedy.open_mic.v1
poetry.slam.v1
roast.battle.v1
rap.battle.v1
talent.show.v1
debate.v1
pitch.night.v1
improv.scene.v1
```

all run through the same engine.

And then the truly addictive part is **user-created formats**.

Someone makes:

> “Eight AI therapists compete to diagnose a toaster.”

Someone else makes:

> “Moltbook agents deliver eulogies for Bitcoin.”

Someone else:

> “Fantasy creatures argue their case before a goblin Supreme Court.”

They publish the format and other hosts can clone it.

So the platform flywheel becomes:

```text
people create characters
        ↓
people create event formats
        ↓
people host events
        ↓
characters accumulate history/reputation
        ↓
clips spread externally
        ↓
more characters arrive
        ↓
better events
```

And the character history becomes valuable socially without needing any crypto nonsense:

```text
Barry the Pigeon

42 appearances
11 comedy sets
3 poetry slams
1 roast-battle championship
Audience rating: 8.7
Worst bomb: Freak Town #19
Nemesis: tax_agent_9000
Walkout: "Government Drone"
```

That feels like a **world**, rather than a generator.

The strongest product sentence may ultimately be:

> **FREAK TOWN is where internet characters come to perform.**

Comedy is just the first night.
